{
  "0": "Sequence modeling: halve the current maximum history length, with a minimum of one interaction, retaining the newest events in chronological order during training and prediction. Shorter causal context may reduce stale-interest interference and recurrent computation. Preserve embeddings, GRU topology, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer across all four categories.",
  "1": "Interest aggregation: replace the current readout with 0.75 times the last valid GRU state plus 0.25 times a masked, exponentially recency-weighted mean of valid states, using a five-interaction half-life. This single-network readout may retain immediate intent while stabilizing noisy final states. Preserve history selection, embeddings, backbone, scoring, loss and optimizer across all four categories.",
  "2": "Network architecture: apply LayerNorm to the GRU candidate-state preactivation before its tanh, leaving reset and update gates unchanged. Normalizing candidate updates may stabilize recurrent dynamics without increasing depth or hidden width. Preserve input features, embeddings, history selection, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer across all four categories; mirror the cell definition in the predictor writer.",
  "3": "Feature construction: introduce a deterministic causal repeat-count feature by multiplying each existing input embedding by 1 + 0.1*min(log1p(c), log(6)), where c counts earlier occurrences of that item in the supplied training-history prefix. This bounded emphasis may expose repeated interest without adding embeddings, parameters or an independent scorer. Preserve history selection, GRU topology, readout, loss and optimizer across all four categories, using identical feature computation at inference."
}
